course: dictionary
lesson: 3215
title: Making words stronger with enormously
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Enormously is an adverb. It means very much, or to a very great amount. It makes other words stronger, so the reader feels how big, how much or how strong something is. «She enjoyed it enormously» means she enjoyed it very much.
explain: «Vain Toad thinks she admires him enormously.» The word enormously tells you how much she admires him: a great deal, not just a little. To check your meaning, swap in very much. «Vain Toad thinks she admires him very much.» It still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
explain: Enormously often goes before an adjective, such as big, long or important. «Enormously big» means hugely, extremely big. Enormously does not change the word it goes with. It only turns up the strength of that word.
fact: Enormously means very much or to a very great amount.
fact: «Enormously big» means hugely, extremely big.
fact: An adverb tells you more about a verb, an adjective or another adverb, for example how much or how.
fact: «Silverfish are wingless insects with three long tails that have looked much the same for an enormously long time.»
fact: Phrases with enormously include enormously important, enormously grateful and enormously popular.
exercise: «She enjoyed the film enormously.» What does enormously mean here? | Quietly | Only a little | Not at all | *Very much
exercise: Which phrase means hugely, extremely big? | Very quiet | *Enormously big | A bit small | Barely big
exercise: In «for an enormously long time», which word makes long stronger? | long | *enormously | insects | time
test: «Vain Toad thinks she admires him enormously.» What is enormously here? | Yesterday | *Very much | Hardly at all | Very small
test: Which word means the same as enormously in «enormously popular»? | Slowly | *Hugely | Quietly | Badly
exam: In «thinks she admires him enormously», what part of speech is enormously? | Verb | Noun | *Adverb | Adjective
